# ==============================================================================
# AquariusOS — KWin defaults for HANDHELDS ONLY
# ==============================================================================
# WHAT THIS FILE IS
#   The same kind of file as ../xdg/kwinrc next door: factory settings for KWin,
#   the part of KDE that draws every window and handles every input device. Read
#   the top of ../xdg/kdeglobals first if the "we add a level, we never edit
#   theirs" arrangement is new to you — it explains the whole search order.
#
#   Everything here is a DEFAULT and never a forced value. Change any of it in
#   System Settings and your choice goes into your own ~/.config/kwinrc, which
#   sits above this file and wins from then on, permanently.
#
# WHY IT IS A SEPARATE FOLDER FROM ../xdg/
#   Because it must reach the handheld image and NOT the two desktop ones, and
#   there is exactly one honest way to do that here: ship the folder, then delete
#   it again on the images it does not belong to. The deletion is in
#   build_files/build.sh, in a `case "$IMAGE_NAME"` next to the one image-info.sh
#   already uses to work out the edition name.
#
#   The folder is put on KDE's search path by the same tiny script as the normal
#   one, ../../../../etc/xdg/plasma-workspace/env/zz-aquarius.sh, which adds it
#   only `if [ -d ... ]`. So on a desktop PC the folder is not there, the `if` is
#   false, and this file has never existed as far as that machine is concerned.
#
# ⚠️ WHAT MUST NEVER GO IN THIS FILE
#   Any `[Wayland]` key. The handheld base image ships its own /etc/xdg/kwinrc
#   with exactly three lines in it, and two of them are the on-screen keyboard:
#
#       [Wayland]
#       InputMethod[$e]=/usr/share/applications/org.kde.plasma.keyboard.desktop
#       VirtualKeyboardEnabled=true
#
#   Those two lines are load-bearing on a device with no keyboard. This file sits
#   ABOVE /etc/xdg in the search order, so a `[Wayland]` key written here would
#   outrank them without saying so. The same rule is already written down in
#   docs/deck-variant-research.md; it applies twice as hard in this file, which
#   only ever runs on the machines that need those lines.
#     Source: ublue-os/bazzite, spec_files/steamdeck-kde-presets/kwinrc — shipped
#     by the `steamdeck-kde-presets` package, which is installed on the handheld
#     image only (`steamdeck-kde-presets-desktop` on the other two, and its spec
#     file deletes kwinrc during install).
# ==============================================================================

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# DRIVE THE DESKTOP WITH THE CONTROLLER
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The problem this solves: an Ally has no mouse, no keyboard and no trackpad. Its
# only pointing devices are two thumbsticks, a gyro and the touchscreen. Leave
# Game Mode for the desktop with nothing plugged in and, until this line, there
# was no reliable way to move a pointer.
#
# The answer turned out to be sitting in Plasma already. KDE Plasma 6.7 added a
# KWin plug-in called "gamecontroller" that turns any game controller the machine
# can see into a mouse and keyboard, at the compositor — the lowest and most
# reliable place it could possibly happen. It is complete, it is shipped, and it
# is switched OFF out of the box:
#
#     { "KPlugin": { "EnabledByDefault": false } }
#
#   Source: KDE/kwin, Plasma/6.7 branch, src/plugins/gamecontroller/metadata.json
#
# The name of the switch is not written down anywhere as a word; it is assembled
# in code. KWin reads the group `[Plugins]` out of kwinrc and, for every plug-in
# it finds, looks for a key made of the plug-in's id with "Enabled" stuck on the
# end:
#
#     const KConfigGroup config(kwinApp()->config(), QStringLiteral("Plugins"));
#     const QString configKey = pluginId + QLatin1StringView("Enabled");
#
#   Source: KDE/kwin, Plasma/6.7, src/pluginmanager.cpp lines 30–37.
#
# The id is `gamecontroller`, from the plug-in's build file
# (`kcoreaddons_add_plugin(gamecontroller ...)`, src/plugins/gamecontroller/
# CMakeLists.txt), which makes the key below `gamecontrollerEnabled`. Both halves
# were read from source rather than copied off a forum, because a misspelled key
# here would do nothing at all and look exactly like a key that works.
#
# WHAT THE BUTTONS DO. These are KWin's own mappings, not ours — the plug-in has
# no settings and the mappings are compiled in. Read from
# src/plugins/gamecontroller/emulatedinputdevice.cpp (Plasma/6.7):
#
#     either thumbstick   move the pointer
#     right trigger (R2)  left click          ← this is "click", not the A button
#     left trigger (L2)   right click
#     A                   Enter
#     B                   Escape / go back
#     Y                   Space
#     X                   toggle the on-screen keyboard  ⚠️ see below
#     D-pad               arrow keys
#     L1 / R1             hold Ctrl / hold Alt
#     Start               the Super key
#     Select              nothing
#
# ⚠️ The X button's job is broken UPSTREAM, and KDE says so in their own comment
# next to it: "TO-DO — toggle Virtual Keyboard not working.. Activated but does
# not show on-screen". Nothing we can write here fixes that; it is a bug in
# Plasma 6.7. The on-screen keyboard still appears on its own when you tap into a
# text box, which is the route the beginner guide teaches.
#   Source: same file, `case BTN_NORTH`.
#
# ⚠️ ONE KNOWN CONFLICT, and it is worth knowing before you go looking for a bug.
# Steam starts automatically on the handheld's desktop and can ALSO turn the
# controller into a mouse, through its own "Desktop Layout". If both are running
# you get one pointer being pushed by two things, which feels like a sticky or
# doubled cursor. Bazzite's own documentation warns about the same clash. The fix
# is to turn one of them off — Steam → Settings → Controller → Desktop Layout —
# and ours is the one to keep, because Steam's is per-account, needs setting up
# by hand, and Bazzite records that it "may not exist by default" on handhelds
# that are not Steam Decks.
#   Source: docs.bazzite.gg, "Common Issues & Resolutions".
#
# Why this is not on the desktop images: on a PC the pointer is the mouse, and a
# controller plugged in for a game would start shoving the cursor around as well.
# That is a nuisance there and a necessity here, which is the whole reason this
# file is handheld-only.
[Plugins]
gamecontrollerEnabled=true

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A GENTLER BLUR, BECAUSE A HANDHELD IS NOT A DESKTOP
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# AquariusOS blurs the background behind every window — see the long section in
# ../xdg/kwinrc, and docs/kwin-effects-layer.md for what the effect is and why
# it is compiled into the image. It is a shader that runs on the graphics chip
# for every window on screen, every frame, and upstream says so themselves:
# "This effect can be very resource-intensive if you have a lot of windows
# open."
#   Source: xarblu/kwin-effects-better-blur-dx, README.md, "Known Issues".
#
# On a desktop with a real graphics card that is free. On a handheld it is not:
# the graphics chip is part of the same processor as everything else, shares the
# same memory and the same power budget, and the machine runs off a battery.
# Softer blur is more passes over the whole screen, so it is the one setting
# here with a direct cost in frames and in minutes of use.
#
# So the handheld gets the same look, one notch cheaper.
#
# HOW ONE LINE HERE CHANGES ONE LINE THERE, AND LEAVES THE REST ALONE
#   KDE reads its settings from a stack of folders and merges them KEY BY KEY,
#   not file by file — the same reason a user changing one thing in System
#   Settings does not lose every other default. This folder sits in FRONT of
#   ../xdg/ in that stack (see the last block of
#   ../../../../etc/xdg/plasma-workspace/env/zz-aquarius.sh, which says it is
#   ordered this way precisely so that the more specific answer wins the day the
#   two overlap). So BlurStrength below replaces the desktop file's 11, and
#   every other blur setting — the saturation, the corner radius, the "blur
#   everything" rule — still comes through from ../xdg/kwinrc underneath.
#
#   That is also why only this ONE key is written here. Copying the whole
#   section across would mean two files to keep in step, and one of them would
#   quietly fall behind.
#
# The section name is spelled with hyphens, which is not a typo — it is what
# the project calls it, in src/blur.kcfg: <group name="Effect-better-blur-dx">.
[Effect-better-blur-dx]

# 6 instead of the desktop's 11, on a 1–15 scale. Still clearly frosted glass,
# roughly half the shader passes.
#
# ⚠️ THIS NUMBER IS A STARTING POINT, NOT A MEASUREMENT. It has never been run
# on Royce's ROG Xbox Ally — nothing in this repo can be, because the build
# machines and his Mac are not handhelds. It is set from the shape of the
# problem, not from a frame-rate capture, and it should be checked on the real
# device: if the desktop still feels heavy, drop it further (4, or 1); if it is
# perfectly smooth, it can go back up towards the desktop's 11.
#
# The honest way to check is to look at the desktop, not at a number — drag a
# window around over the wallpaper with a few others open and see whether it
# keeps up. Game Mode is not affected either way: it runs on gamescope, which
# is a different compositor with no KWin effects in it at all.
BlurStrength=6

# The grain is off on the desktop image too, so this is not a handheld-specific
# choice — it is only repeated because a person reading this file should not
# have to open another one to know whether it applies. It costs a little GPU
# time for an effect our flat surfaces do not need.
NoiseStrength=0
